(11) Truckstop carrier onboarding product (monitoring and automation): https://truckstop.com/product/carrier-onboarding/
(12) TIA 2024 Framework to Combat Fraud: https://news.tianet.org/2024-tia-framework-to-combat-fraud/

**SEA-specific lens:** In Southeast Asia, the small-carrier vetting problem is structurally worse and has no market-grade mitigation. Singapore LTA DataMall publishes aggregate goods-vehicle registration statistics (vehicle counts by type and make) but no carrier compliance scores, safety ratings, or authority-change alerts equivalent to FMCSA SAFER. Indonesia Kemenhub maintains a data portal (portaldata.kemenhub.go.id) but no publicly queryable motor-carrier safety database has been confirmed. Malaysia JPJ regulates vehicle and operator licensing but no centralized freight-carrier safety-score registry exists. The practical result: SEA shippers and mid-market 3PLs below multinational tier rely on marketplace platform pre-qualification (Kargo Technologies in Indonesia, Ezyhaul in Singapore/Malaysia) or informal reference checks. Neither marketplace provides an externally queryable vetting API; both vet only their own carrier pools. A shipper using a carrier outside these platforms has zero institutional vetting support. [ASSUMED-1]: SEA mid-market shippers and 3PLs perform no structured carrier vetting beyond requesting a photocopy of a vehicle license and calling a reference — to validate: walk through the full carrier onboarding checklist with 10 mid-market SEA shippers in Indonesia, Malaysia, and Thailand; document every data point verified and which registry (if any) is queried.

**Why current solutions fall short:**
- **Highway, Descartes MCP, Carrier411:** Cover only FMCSA-registered US carriers; zero functionality for SEA. Within the US, adoption falls off sharply below the top-100-broker tier; the long tail of ~12,000+ active US freight brokers is underserved.
- **TrustLayer, myCOI:** COI management only — do not surface carrier safety scores or fraud signals.
- **Refinitiv World-Check, Sayari:** KYC/beneficial-ownership tools; not carrier-safety-native; enterprise pricing puts them out of reach for mid-market brokers.
- **Coupa, SAP Ariba, JAGGAER:** No native FMCSA/CSA data integration; enterprise shippers using these platforms assume vetting is complete when it is not.
- **Kargo, Ezyhaul (SEA):** Internal carrier pool only; no externally-sold vetting API; no safety-scoring framework beyond national licensing check.

**Field validation questions:**
- [INTERVIEW: compliance director at a TIA-member broker with $50M–$200M annual volume] — "Walk me through your carrier onboarding checklist for a spot load. Which databases do you query, in what sequence, and who does it?"
- [INTERVIEW: operations manager at a SEA-based 3PL with $10M+ annual freight spend] — "What does your onboarding process look like when you engage a new sub-carrier you've never used before? What documents do you request and which government registries, if any, do you check?"
- [INTERVIEW: risk/legal officer at a mid-market US broker] — "Has the Montgomery v. Caribe ruling changed your carrier vetting investment or documentation standards? Have you received guidance from your insurer?"
- [INTERVIEW: head of carrier compliance at a top-50 US broker using Highway or Descartes MCP] — "What percentage of your carrier onboardings trigger a hold or rejection from the platform, and what are the most common failure reasons?"
- [INTERVIEW: product lead at Highway or Descartes MCP] — "What percentage of your signed customers are below the top-100-broker tier, and what is the primary objection from brokers who don't buy?"

