### Pain point 1.2: Double brokering and MC-identity fraud are escalating faster than the detection layer

**Description:** Freight brokers accept loads from carriers that appear FMCSA-legitimate — correct MC authority, active status, matching insurance on file — but are in fact fraud rings operating through harvested or purchased MC numbers, compromised carrier email accounts, or identity impersonation. By the time the fraud is detected (typically when the legitimate carrier calls asking about an unpaid load, or cargo fails to arrive), the freight has been cross-docked, resold, or moved overseas. The fraud is structurally undetectable by legacy carrier-vetting tools because the fraudulent carrier presents as a real, clean FMCSA entity. Only real-time identity-change monitoring — tracking FMCSA contact-info updates, phone number changes, and email domain freshness — can catch it before tender. No such capability exists outside the US, and within the US it is adopted by fewer than 10% of active brokers.

**Who bears it:** Freight broker (primary loss bearer: undelivered load + potential cargo claim + lost shipper relationship); shipper (cargo loss, supply chain disruption); legitimate carrier whose identity was hijacked (reputational harm, load disputes).

**Estimated cost:**
- **Industry-wide annual loss (TIA estimate):** $500M–$700M per year in US freight fraud including double brokering and financial theft; TIA President cited potential to surpass $800M at current growth rates. Inbound Logistics sources put 2025 supply chain crime losses at $725M, a 60% increase from 2024. (1)(2)
- **Per-incident average:** Verisk CargoNet reports the average cargo theft loss per incident at $202,364 in 2024 (up from $187,895 in 2023); strategic theft incidents that involve identity fraud are typically higher-value targets. (3)
- **Prevention cost (broker level):** TIA survey: 10% of respondents spent $200,000+ on fraud prevention in six months — an annualized $400,000+ prevention spend for the most active defensive operators. (4)
- **Underground MC number market price:** One documented case shows MC authorities transacted for $20,000 in cash; bulk-harvested authorities by organized rings are priced lower per unit, enabling scale. (5)
- **FMCSA enforcement coverage gap:** FMCSA's $965M budget covers 9.2 million drivers and 2 million registered entities; only ~12,300 compliance reviews were conducted in 2024 — 1.5% of active carriers receiving any investigator contact. Reactive enforcement means fraud rings have hours to complete a load theft before detection is even possible. (5)

**Willingness to pay:** Not sourced. Highway's network growth (70 of top 100 US brokers as of August 2025) and FTV Capital growth equity round are market signals; Truckstop Risk Factors' FreightWaves 2025 Fraud Fighter Award and reported 57% fraud-incident reduction for users signals WTP validation at scale. WTP for mid-market: structurally similar to Pain point 1.1 — constrained by margin, elevated by post-*Montgomery* liability. [INTERVIEW: ask highway.com or Truckstop Risk Factors product team for customer-segment breakdown and average contract value.]

**Frequency:**
- Highway blocked 1,986,995 fraudulent email attempts in full-year 2025, up 117% from 914,719 in 2024 — the most direct measure of attack frequency against broker networks. (6)
- Q2 2025: 135% spike in suspicious MC ownership changes connected to strategic cargo theft reports (Highway). (7)
- Q1 2025: three primary fraud vectors spiked — sold MCs/ownership changes, compromised inbox/email phishing, phone spoofing. (8)
- TIA Watchdog: 1,611 fraud reports filed September 2024 – February 2025 alone, a 65% increase vs. prior eight-month period. (4)
- **Strategic cargo theft** — the sub-category covering identity fraud, fictitious pickups, and double-brokering schemes — has surged nearly 1,500% since 2021 per CargoNet (Verisk); this is the fraud-driven sub-segment, not total cargo theft incidents. Overall recorded incidents grew from ~1,546 (2021) to 3,625 (2024), a 135% cumulative increase. (2)(3)
- Verisk CargoNet 2024 dollar losses reached a record $454.9M, a 60% YoY increase from 2023's $331.9M. (3)
- BSI/TT Club 2024 Cargo Theft Report (covering 2023 US data) documents fictitious thefts — the strategic-theft category that includes brokering fraud — rising from 1% to 17% of all US cargo thefts between 2022 and 2023, with double/triple-brokering schemes named as a contributing tactic alongside identity theft. (9)
- Truckstop (US load-board platform) recorded a 400% spike in double-brokering complaints from Q4 2022 through Q1 2023, attributed to Brent Hutto (Chief Relationship Officer); this is platform-complaint volume, not a regional incident figure. (13)

**Evidenced or assumed:**
(1) TIA freight fraud losses — Trucking Dive coverage: https://www.truckingdive.com/news/brokerage-fraud-costs-could-surpass-800-million-dollars-transportation-intermediaries-association/650595/
(2) Strategic cargo theft up nearly 1,500% since 2021 per CargoNet (Verisk), cited via ATS Logistics summary of CargoNet data: https://www.atsinc.com/blog/cargo-theft-rising-protect-shipments (cross-referenced: Inbound Logistics https://www.inboundlogistics.com/articles/risky-business-inside-the-freight-fraud-surge/ ; FreightWaves https://www.freightwaves.com/news/strategic-cargo-theft-growth-pushes-industry-fraud-toward-all-time-high). CargoNet primary annual report (member-gated): https://www.cargonet.com/news-and-events/cargonet-in-the-media/2024-theft-trends/
(3) Verisk CargoNet 2024 cargo theft record — Verisk press release: https://www.verisk.com/company/newsroom/cargo-theft-surges-to-record-levels-in-2024-verisk-cargonet-analysis-reveals/
(4) TIA State of Fraud April 2025 report summary: https://news.tianet.org/tia-releases-state-of-fraud-in-the-industry-april-2025-report/
(5) FreightWaves — underground market for MC numbers: https://www.freightwaves.com/news/catch-me-if-you-can-the-underground-market-for-mc-numbers-regulators-are-trying-to-stop
