### Pain point 1.3: COI verification is structurally post-hoc — carriers go uninsured mid-shipment and nobody knows

**Description:** The industry default for confirming a carrier's insurance coverage remains PDF-based: a carrier provides an ACORD 25 Certificate of Insurance at onboarding, the broker files it, and compliance is assumed to persist until the next annual renewal. Three structural failures cascade from this process: (a) policies can be cancelled mid-term for non-payment — and insurers are legally required to notify only the insured, not certificate holders; (b) COI documents are trivially forgeable — "in under five minutes using freely available ACORD 25 templates and basic PDF editing software"; (c) a static document cannot reflect the five or more policy changes (equipment adds/removals, coverage limit adjustments, pending cancellations) that occur annually on an average carrier policy. The result: a broker can be dispatching loads on coverage that has not existed for weeks.

**Who bears it:** Freight broker (uninsured carrier leaves broker contingently liable); shipper (cargo loss denial at claim time if carrier policy lapsed); insurer (unexpected subrogation exposure); small carrier (policy lapse often due to cash-flow pressure, carrier unaware until a claim surfaces).

**Estimated cost:**
- **Single uninsured claim example:** Certificial documents a $162,000 uninsured claim where a fraudulent COI bypassed broker verification. Legal cases (Westchester County Supreme Court) confirm General Contractors forced to cover worker accidents personally when subcontractor COI proved fake. (1)
- **Broker contingent cargo liability:** When a carrier's primary insurance is absent, cancelled, or insufficient, the liability falls to the broker — particularly if the broker contracted with the shipper to carry cargo liability, or if the new Montgomery standard exposes broker selection negligence. Contingent cargo policies exist to bridge this gap but are not universally held. (2)(3)
- **Manual compliance labor cost:** Tracking and re-requesting COIs from a network of active carriers is estimated at 0.5–1.5 FTE-hours per carrier per annual renewal cycle; platforms like myCOI and TrustLayer claim to reduce this by 60–80% through automated collection and AI extraction. (4)(5)
- **Legacy document-based tools achieve 60–70% compliance rates on average versus 90%+ on network-based verification platforms** — a 20–30 percentage point accuracy gap translating directly into undetected coverage lapses per source: Certificial product documentation. (1)
- **OFAC/legal compliance cost for freight forwarders using inadequate screening:** Fracht FWO (Texas-based freight forwarder) received a $1.6M OFAC penalty in 2025 for failing to establish adequate controls for high-risk cargo transactions — illustrative of the regulatory cost of compliance gaps at the verification layer. (6)

**Willingness to pay:** Not sourced at the freight-specific level. TrustLayer raised $15M Series A (2021) with estimated ~$3.8M ARR by 2024 (Latka, low confidence); myCOI raised ~$10M in total funding. Both are multi-industry platforms not freight-native — actual freight WTP would require broker-segment interviews. [INTERVIEW: ask compliance directors at three mid-market brokers whether COI management is a budget line item, and what they currently pay for it.]

**Frequency:**
- Policies change approximately 5 times annually per vendor (equipment adds/removals, coverage adjustments, pending cancellation events) — meaning an annual COI re-collection cycle is structurally inadequate and misses the majority of coverage changes. (7)
- A carrier can provide a valid COI on Monday and have their policy cancelled for non-payment by Wednesday — documented in industry legal guidance but not quantified by frequency surveys. (2)
- Vendor-submitted COIs represent "the highest fraud risk" because they bypass insurance industry verification chains entirely; no industry-wide fraud-rate estimate confirmed. (1)
- Certificial is the only platform with live-AMS integration verification; the rest of the market (myCOI, TrustLayer, RMIS) parses static documents and cannot detect mid-contract policy cancellations until the next renewal cycle per value-chain-mapper assessment.

**Evidenced or assumed:**
(1) Certificial — COI fraud detection guide, $162,000 uninsured claim example, 60-70% vs 90%+ compliance rate: https://www.certificial.com/blog-post/how-to-detect-fraudulent-certificates-of-insurance-complete-coi-verification-guide
(2) Certificial — mid-term policy cancellation, 5 policy changes/year, no certificate-holder notice obligation: https://www.certificial.com/blog-post/what-happens-when-your-vendors-insurance-policy-is-cancelled-mid-term
(3) Freight 360 — broker liability when carrier insurance fails, contingent cargo policy need: https://www.freight360.net/essential-guide-verifying-motor-carrier-insurance-for-freight-brokers/
(4) myCOI — COI tracking and automation overview: https://mycoitracking.com/5-things-to-verify-your-coi-is-valid/
(5) TrustLayer Series A — $15M COI automation: https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/trustlayer-raises-15m-to-build-the-future-of-insurance-verification-301357564.html
(6) OFAC penalties for freight forwarders 2025 — Fracht FWO $1.6M: https://blog.gettransport.com/news/ofac-enforcement-activities-2025/
(7) Broker liability after Montgomery ruling — insurance exposure, no adequate bond: https://beinsure.com/news/freight-brokers-face-new-insurance-gap-after-court-ruling/
(8) NewArk Agency — COI requirements for freight brokers 2026 guide: https://www.newarkagency.com/blog_pages/certificate-of-insurance-coi-requirements-for-freight-brokers-2026-guide
(9) Resistant.AI — how to spot fake COI: https://resistant.ai/blog/how-to-spot-fake-certificates-of-insurance
