(12) Certificial platform homepage (live AMS verification model): https://www.certificial.com/

**Root Causes**

- **RC1: Insurance law imposes no notification obligation on insurers to certificate holders, creating a structural information gap that cannot be closed by contract alone.** Under New York Insurance Law (and parallel statutes in most US states), an insurer cancelling a policy must notify only the insured and the insured's authorized broker — not the certificate holder, even if named as an additional insured. Courts have upheld this rule explicitly; one New York court rejected an additional insured's argument that they deserved cancellation notice, citing the COI's own disclaimer language. This is not a technology problem — it is a legal structure that insulates the insurer from any duty to certificate holders. The only technical solution that closes the gap is real-time integration with the insurer's own agency management system (AMS) — which Certificial does, but which requires the insurer or agency to have an AMS integration partner relationship with the verification platform. The limiting factor is AMS integration breadth, not carrier willingness to be monitored.

- **RC2: The ACORD 25 form is a marketing document, not a binding insurance certificate — its disclaimer language explicitly limits its legal weight, but the industry treats it as evidence of coverage.** The ACORD 25 "Certificate of Insurance" includes boilerplate language stating: "This certificate is issued as a matter of information only and confers no rights upon the certificate holder." Nonetheless, the freight industry has made ACORD 25 collection the compliance standard for carrier insurance verification. This creates a compliance theater dynamic: brokers check the box ("we have a COI on file"), but the document they hold is legally non-binding and can be produced by any party with a PDF editor. The gap persists because the document is costless to produce, universally recognized, and — until a claim — never tested.

- **RC3: No US regulatory standard requires brokers to verify COI authenticity at onboarding, only to collect it — incentivizing the minimum-cost collection approach.** FMCSA requires that brokers ensure carriers maintain required insurance and authority; the practical implementation standard in the industry is "collect a COI." There is no regulatory mandate to verify the COI against the insurer's own records, verify that the named carrier matches the operating authority, or confirm coverage limits are current. This regulatory minimum creates a race-to-minimum in carrier onboarding: brokers who invest in real-time AMS verification are spending more than required while competing with brokers who collect a PDF and move on. Only post-*Montgomery* liability creates a private law incentive to exceed the regulatory floor.

- **RC4: Freight carrier insurance is a high-turnover, cash-flow-sensitive product for owner-operators, making mid-term cancellations structurally frequent — but the monitoring infrastructure was designed for annual renewal cycles.** Owner-operators (91.5% of active US carriers operate 10 or fewer trucks) often purchase short-term or pay-as-you-go insurance policies tied directly to operating revenue. When freight rates decline (as they did in 2023–2024's prolonged freight recession), premium payments lapse first. Insurance brokers report that mid-term cancellations for non-payment are most common in the owner-operator segment — precisely the segment most active in spot-market brokered loads. The annual COI renewal workflow was designed for commercial fleets with stable long-term policies; applying it to the spot-market carrier universe creates a structural verification lag.

**SEA-specific lens:** In Southeast Asia, no regulatory analog to FMCSA's Form H insurance filing requirement exists at the national level for road freight. Singapore LTA requires third-party liability insurance for all vehicles but does not maintain a public real-time registry of carrier insurance coverage status queryable by brokers or shippers. Indonesia requires carrier liability insurance as part of vehicle registration but the verification workflow is paper-based and registry-dependent rather than real-time. The COI fraud vector is less prominent in SEA because marketplace platforms (Kargo, Ezyhaul) intermediate the insurance liability question — carriers must hold valid national insurance to register on the platform, and platform renewal checks serve as a proxy. However, this creates a false-compliance surface: a carrier can lapse mid-contract and the marketplace has no obligation to notify the shipper. SEA shippers using carriers outside marketplace platforms have no structured COI verification workflow at all. [ASSUMED-3]: SEA shippers tendering loads to sub-tier carriers outside marketplace platforms do not request or verify insurance documentation at onboarding — to validate: ask five Indonesian or Thai shippers what insurance documentation they collect from new carriers and how they confirm it is current.

**Field validation questions:**
- [INTERVIEW: claims manager at a cargo insurer active in US freight] — "What percentage of cargo claims you process involve a carrier whose insurance had lapsed or was cancelled before the incident? Can you share anonymized case data?"
- [INTERVIEW: compliance officer at a mid-market US broker with 500+ active carriers] — "How do you currently discover that a carrier's COI has lapsed mid-contract? How many times in the past year did you discover a lapse before a loss versus after?"
- [INTERVIEW: product manager at Certificial] — "What is your current carrier coverage (what percentage of US for-hire carriers are connected to an agency with an AMS integration)? What is the limiting factor to expanding coverage?"

---

### Pain point 1.4: No SEA equivalent to FMCSA — all US carrier-vetting infrastructure is geographically stranded

**Description:** Every major US carrier-vetting platform — Highway, Descartes MyCarrierPortal, Carrier411, SaferWatch, DAT CarrierWatch, Truckstop Risk Factors, CarrierAssure — is architecturally dependent on FMCSA's SAFER system data: carrier registration, authority status, CSA safety scores, insurance filings, and the contact-change events that Highway instruments as fraud signals. FMCSA SAFER is a US-only database for US-registered interstate carriers. Singapore LTA, Indonesia Kemenhub, Malaysia JPJ, Thailand DLT, Vietnam MoT, and the Philippines LTO each maintain separate national licensing registries. None is publicly queryable via structured API with the equivalent data depth of FMCSA SAFER. None has a carrier safety-scoring framework analogous to CSA BASIC scores. There is no ASEAN-level motor carrier database. The practical consequence: a shipper in Singapore tendering a cross-border road freight move to Malaysia cannot run any structured carrier-safety or fraud-signal check — they rely on word of mouth, marketplace pre-qualification, or manual license verification.

**Who bears it:** ASEAN-headquartered shippers and 3PLs; multinational manufacturers and retailers sourcing carriers in SEA (e.g., FMCG companies using Indonesian or Thai 3PLs); cargo insurers pricing SEA road freight risk; freight forwarders managing last-mile in SEA markets.

**Estimated cost:**
- **SEA road freight market exposure:** ASEAN road freight transport market estimated at $119.4B in 2025 (Mordor Intelligence, medium confidence); cargo moving through this market has zero structured carrier vetting infrastructure available. (1)
- **SEA cargo theft — Asia share:** Indonesia accounted for 14% of all Asia-region cargo theft incidents in BSI/TT Club data; Vietnam 3%; insider involvement in Asian theft incidents at 13%, higher than global average. (2) No SEA-specific per-incident loss value comparable to CargoNet's US data is published — this is itself a data gap.
- **Insurer pricing premium for unverifiable risk:** [ASSUMED-4]: Cargo insurers applying freight risk policies for SEA road freight apply a higher rate loading for Southeast Asian road freight vs. comparable European freight because carrier safety data is unavailable — to validate: ask underwriting teams at TT Club, Allianz Trade, or Zurich about the rate differential for SEA road freight and the named driver of that differential.
- **Gap in platform revenue:** No third-party carrier-vetting SaaS platform has raised visible capital to serve the SEA market. The absence of commercial entrants, despite a $119.4B road freight market, is structural evidence of a market not yet addressable — either because the data layer (government registries) is not accessible, or because the willingness to pay has not been validated.

**Willingness to pay:** Not sourced. [ASSUMED-5]: Enterprise shippers (multinational FMCG and manufacturing companies) operating in Indonesia, Thailand, and Vietnam would pay $500–$2,000/month for a SEA-specific carrier compliance monitoring tool if one existed with API connectivity to national registries — to validate: ask five SEA-market procurement and supply chain directors at multinational manufacturers what they currently spend on carrier risk management in SEA and whether they would fund a new solution.

**Frequency:**
- Singapore LTA DataMall confirms: available freight-related datasets cover only aggregate vehicle population counts (annual goods vehicle population by type/weight); no carrier safety score, inspection record, or violation history is available to third parties. (3)
- Indonesia Kemenhub portal (portaldata.kemenhub.go.id) maintains transport sector datasets; no publicly queryable motor carrier safety database equivalent to FMCSA SAFER has been confirmed despite direct search. (4)
- Malaysia JPJ: no centralized freight-carrier safety-score database confirmed per value-chain research; carrier vetting standard is national vehicle/operator license check only. [ASSUMED-6]: JPJ database is not programmatically accessible to third parties — to validate: contact JPJ directly and request API documentation for commercial carrier compliance queries.
- Vietnam MoT, Thailand DLT, Philippines LTO: equivalent national licensing exists but no evidence of a structured public API for carrier safety data found.
- Kargo Technologies (Indonesia's largest B2B trucking marketplace, 40,000+ trucks) and Ezyhaul (Singapore, five-country SEA) perform internal carrier vetting for their own pools but sell no vetting API externally. (5)(6)

**Evidenced or assumed:**
(1) Mordor Intelligence, ASEAN Road Freight Transport Market 2025: https://www.mordorintelligence.com/industry-reports/asean-road-freight-transport-market
(2) BSI/TT Club 2024 Cargo Theft Report — Asia breakdown (India 63%, Indonesia 14%, China 11%, Vietnam 3%), 13% insider participation: https://www.ttclub.com/news-and-resources/publications/article/bsi-consulting-and-tt-club-2024-cargo-theft-report/
(3) Singapore LTA DataMall static datasets (goods vehicle population only, no safety scoring): https://datamall.lta.gov.sg/content/datamall/en/static-data.html
(4) Indonesia Kemenhub data portal (no structured motor carrier safety API confirmed): https://portaldata.kemenhub.go.id/
(5) Kargo Technologies — Indonesia's largest B2B trucking marketplace (40,000+ trucks, internal vetting): https://www.kargo.tech/en/home-indonesia
(6) Ezyhaul — Singapore HQ, five-country SEA, internal carrier pre-qualification: https://theloadstar.com/ezyhaul-shell-tie-up-the-latest-start-up-in-growing-asia-digital-road-freight-sector/
(7) Singapore LTA goods vehicle and engineering plant requirements: https://onemotoring.lta.gov.sg/content/onemotoring/home/buying/vehicle-types-and-registrations/commercial-vehicle/goods-vehicle-and-engineering-plant.html
(8) Singapore LTA DataMall API User Guide (confirms traffic/transit focus, no freight carrier compliance data): https://datamall.lta.gov.sg/content/dam/datamall/datasets/LTA_DataMall_API_User_Guide.pdf
(9) FMCSA SAFER system overview (US-only scope): https://safer.fmcsa.dot.gov/
(10) FMCSA 2024 Pocket Guide — 787,000 active carriers, 91.5% operating ≤10 trucks, basis for comparison with SEA: https://www.fmcsa.dot.gov/safety/data-and-statistics/2024-pocket-guide-large-truck-and-bus-statistics
(11) Kargo Technologies acquires TheLorry, expands to Malaysia: https://newsroom.airasia.com/news/teleport-invests-in-indonesias-largest-trucking-marketplace-kargo-technologies
(12) ComplyAdvantage — financial crime risks for APAC shipping firms, regional compliance gap: https://complyadvantage.com/insights/financial-crime-risks-shipping/

**Root Causes**

- **RC1: ASEAN has no supranational freight transport authority with a mandate to build a common carrier-data layer.** FMCSA exists because the US Interstate Commerce Act created federal authority over interstate motor carriers — a single legal framework covering all 50 states. ASEAN's treaty framework explicitly preserves national regulatory sovereignty; transport licensing is a member-state function. The ASEAN Framework Agreement on the Facilitation of Goods in Transit (AFAFGIT) addresses cross-border movement but does not create shared carrier safety standards or a unified registry. Without a supranational mandate and the budget to execute it, no regional carrier database can be built by government action. And without a government-created data layer, private platforms cannot build vetting tools equivalent to those built on FMCSA SAFER.

- **RC2: Individual SEA government transport registries were built for compliance administration, not third-party access, and the political incentive to open them to commercial use is absent.** Singapore LTA DataMall, Indonesia Kemenhub, Malaysia JPJ, Thailand DLT, and Philippines LTO each maintain licensing and registration databases. These systems were architected for internal administrative use — issuing licenses, recording renewals, tracking violations — not for real-time commercial API consumption. Opening them to commercial third parties requires regulatory reform, API infrastructure investment, and a revenue/data-sharing model. None of these governments has a visible program to do this for freight carrier data specifically (Singapore LTA DataMall focuses on public transit, traffic, and passenger mobility data, not freight carrier safety). The political economy of opening carrier compliance data to private platforms is complicated by concerns about competitive use of government data.

- **RC3: SEA's freight market digitization layer is marketplace-embedded, not regtech-embedded — solving for transaction efficiency but not for counterparty risk.** Kargo, Ezyhaul, Logivan, Deliveree, and other digital freight platforms solved the capacity-matching problem (connecting shippers to available trucks) and built internal carrier vetting for their own pools as a product quality feature. They have not productized carrier vetting as a standalone, externally-sellable regtech layer — because their business model captures value through freight transaction intermediation, not through compliance data licensing. This means SEA's most technically sophisticated carriers are inside walled gardens. Shippers who go outside the marketplace (to access price competition, larger vehicles, or specialized equipment) revert immediately to zero formal vetting.

- **RC4: The SEA freight market's concentration of informal operators makes a government-registry-based vetting approach structurally insufficient even if the registries were opened.** A meaningful fraction of road freight in Indonesia, Vietnam, and parts of Thailand and Philippines is moved by unregistered or partially-registered operators — vehicles without current road tax, drivers without full commercial licenses, carriers without formal corporate registration. Any vetting tool that queries only formal registries would generate false-clean results for informal operators who appear clean by virtue of not appearing at all. Building a complete carrier-risk picture in SEA markets would require non-government data sources (marketplace transaction history, telemetry data, community reporting) layered on top of whatever government data is available — a harder and more expensive data-integration problem than the US FMCSA-based model.

**SEA-specific lens:** This pain point is the SEA problem. There is no analog US comparison — the US FMCSA infrastructure, built over 30 years of federal motor carrier regulation, has no parallel in any SEA market. The specific gap is: (a) no structured public API from any SEA national registry provides carrier safety scores or fraud signals; (b) no commercial third-party platform has raised capital to build SEA-specific carrier vetting; (c) marketplace pre-qualification (Kargo, Ezyhaul) covers only carriers inside those platforms' own networks. BSI/TT Club cargo theft data confirms Asia-region theft is real (Indonesia second in Asia at 14% of incidents), but no SEA-specific carrier identity fraud dataset exists — itself a data gap that reflects the absence of the monitoring infrastructure that would generate such data.

**Field validation questions:**
- [INTERVIEW: supply chain director at a multinational FMCG company (Unilever, Nestlé, P&G) with manufacturing in Indonesia or Thailand] — "How do you vet the sub-tier truckers that your 3PLs use for last-mile distribution? What information do you request and which government source, if any, does your 3PL query?"
- [INTERVIEW: product or compliance lead at Kargo Technologies or Ezyhaul] — "Would you sell your carrier vetting data and methodology as a standalone API to shippers who are not your customers? What would need to be true for that to make sense commercially?"
- [INTERVIEW: freight insurance underwriter at TT Club APAC or Allianz Trade Singapore] — "Do you apply a rate loading for SEA road freight because of the absence of carrier safety data? What would you pay for a structured SEA carrier compliance data feed?"
- [INTERVIEW: government transport official at Singapore LTA or Indonesia Kemenhub] — "Is there any initiative to open road freight carrier safety data to commercial third parties? Who in government owns that question?"

