[ASSUMED-4]: Spot-market loads carry materially higher theft exposure than contracted-lane loads due to carrier anonymity and compressed verification windows — the "3x theft rate" working hypothesis is not confirmed in any named primary source. To validate: request from Verisk CargoNet and BSI whether their incident database codes loads by procurement channel (spot vs. contract); if not coded, commission primary research with three or more cargo insurers to segment claims by procurement channel.

[ASSUMED-5]: Spot-market loads represent 15–25% of total US truckload volume in the current freight cycle — to validate: cross-reference DAT SONAR spot-vs-contract mix data with Armstrong & Associates 3PL segmentation.

[ASSUMED-6]: Adoption of per-load insurance on DAT (via Loadsure integration) is below 10% of eligible spot loads — to validate: request adoption rate data from Loadsure or DAT; alternatively, survey 20 brokers on how frequently they purchase per-load insurance on spot loads.

[ASSUMED-7]: Freight brokers would adopt TMS-native per-load cargo insurance binding if the premium could be passed through to the shipper or deducted from the load margin with one click — to validate: ask three McLeod/Aljex users whether they use the Loadsure integration, what percentage of loads they insure per-load vs. relying on annual policy, and what friction prevents higher adoption.

[ASSUMED-8]: The majority of mid-market SEA shippers who use trucks outside major marketplace platforms have no per-load or per-shipment cargo insurance coverage — to validate: survey five mid-market SEA shippers on their cargo insurance workflow for non-marketplace carrier tendering.

[ASSUMED-9]: Cargo insurers apply a higher rate loading for SEA road freight versus comparable EU freight because carrier safety data is unavailable for underwriting — to validate: ask underwriting teams at TT Club APAC, Allianz Trade Singapore, and Zurich Insurance about the rate differential.

[ASSUMED-10]: Enterprise multinational shippers operating in Indonesia, Thailand, and Vietnam would pay $500–$2,000/month for a SEA-specific carrier compliance monitoring tool usable at the tender step — to validate: ask five SEA-market procurement and logistics directors at multinational manufacturers what they currently spend on carrier risk management in SEA.


