## Stage 3 — In-transit pain points

## Stage 3: In-Transit — Visibility & Monitoring

---

### Pain point 3.1: RTTVP signal loss at the small-carrier integration threshold

**Description:** Real-time transportation visibility platforms — project44, FourKites, Shippeo — achieve strong tracking compliance for large carriers connected via ELD API or EDI, but when a 3PL subcontracts to the long tail of small owner-operators, visibility reverts to manual check-ins, app-based pings, or no data at all. The industry average visibility compliance rate hovers at 30–40% even as shippers demand 80–90% compliance from their broker partners, creating a structural 40–50 percentage point gap. This gap concentrates precisely at the tier of carriers that executes a majority of brokered spot loads: in the US, 97% of active trucking companies operate 20 trucks or fewer, and 90% operate six or fewer. These carriers are asked to install a new single-function load tracking app for every broker relationship, generating app fatigue that suppresses ongoing compliance. project44 acknowledged the problem explicitly enough to launch its Network Operations Agent in April 2026 — an AI system to automatically detect and resolve carrier connectivity gaps, missing equipment IDs, and incomplete milestone data — which is direct product evidence that the problem is unresolved at scale.

**Who bears it:** Mid-market freight brokers (primary compliance liability to shippers); shippers contracting through brokers for spot capacity; 3PLs whose carrier networks include owner-operators not integrated with RTTVP networks; cargo insurers pricing in-transit loss without reliable location data.

**Estimated cost:**
- **Broker revenue at risk from compliance failures:** Shippers who require 80–90% visibility compliance penalize or terminate broker relationships falling below threshold. At industry average compliance of 30–40%, the majority of brokers managing spot loads are structurally at risk. Annual US truck brokerage revenue exceeds $80B (ATA/Armstrong & Associates); even a 1–2% broker share loss from compliance failures equals $800M–$1.6B in broker revenue exposure. (1)(2)
- **Project44 carrier managed services premium:** project44's own Carrier Managed Services offering claims 20–40 percentage point improvements in data availability and 40–70 percentage point improvements in milestone completeness — priced as a premium add-on above the base platform license. The existence of a paid remediation layer confirms the base platform does not solve the small-carrier gap. Enterprise RTTVP contracts range from $150,000 to $500,000+ per year; managed services adds 20–40% on top. (3)(4)
- **Manual labor cost of visibility gap management:** When automated pings fail, broker operations teams make manual carrier check-in calls. One mid-market broker (Lipsey Logistics) increased carrier compliance by 20 percentage points through platform adoption — implying that before adoption, broker staff absorbed the compliance shortfall as labor cost. At 3–5 minutes per manual call and 500 loads/day for a mid-market broker, that equals 25–42 FTE-hours per day at $25–$40/hour, or $230,000–$615,000 per year in manual tracking labor. (5)
- **Cargo loss from undetected dwell-time and route deviation:** When visibility fails, theft and route-deviation events go undetected until delivery failure. CargoNet reports average cargo theft loss per incident at $202,364 in 2024 (up from $187,895 in 2023). At Verisk CargoNet's 2024 total of $454.9M in recorded US losses, and with strategic cargo theft increasingly correlated with identity fraud at the small-carrier tier, the visibility gap is a direct enabling condition for the theft vector. (6)(7)

**Willingness to pay:** Not sourced at the broker tier for RTTVP specifically. Indirect signal: project44's $210M+ FY2024 revenue growing at 30%+ YoY, with 1,300+ enterprise customers, shows strong enterprise WTP. Mid-market broker WTP constrained by margin pressure — the segment most affected (brokers with $10M–$100M volume) is least likely to absorb six-figure RTTVP contracts. [Interview placeholder: ask operations directors at 5 mid-market brokers what their current visibility platform spend is and what their compliance rate report shows for their bottom-quartile carrier tier.]

**Frequency:**
- Industry average visibility compliance rate: 30–40% per Trucker Tools published benchmarks; shippers demand 80–90%. Gap = 40–50 percentage points on brokered loads. (1)
- 97% of US trucking companies operate 20 trucks or fewer; 90% operate six or fewer — the carrier population below the ELD API integration threshold for platform-native tracking. (8)
- project44 launched Network Operations Agent (April 2026) after detecting that "nearly one million automated carrier communications" were needed to resolve visibility gaps in its existing 259,000-carrier network — implying an ongoing gap rate on the order of millions of shipments per year. (3)
- project44 G2 data quality rating: 93% vs. category average 88% — the 7-point lead over a fragmented field where some platforms score below 88% confirms data quality variance is structural, not solved. (9)
- Shippers requiring ≥80% compliance: majority of enterprise shippers per Trucker Tools benchmark data; LTI Delivers achieved 86% compliance only after platform adoption (implying pre-platform compliance was below 80%). (1)

**Evidenced or assumed:**
(1) Trucker Tools visibility compliance benchmarks — industry average 30–40%, shipper demand 80–90%, LTI Delivers case study: https://www.truckertools.com/how-to-improve-freight-visibility-compliance/
(2) Armstrong & Associates US truck brokerage market data: https://www.3plogistics.com/3pl-market-info/3pl-market-research/u-s-third-party-logistics-3pl-market-size-estimates/
(3) project44 Network Operations Agent launch (April 2026) — carrier connectivity gaps, milestone data: https://www.globenewswire.com/news-release/2026/04/07/3269228/0/en/project44-Launches-Network-Operations-Agent-to-maintain-visibility-compliance-and-improve-data-accuracy.html
(4) project44 Carrier Managed Services — 20–40pp data availability improvement, 40–70pp milestone improvement: https://www.project44.com/blog/achieving-high-carrier-data-quality-with-project44s-carrier-managed-services/
(5) Descartes Lipsey Logistics case study — 20pp visibility compliance improvement post-adoption: https://www.descartes.com/resources/knowledge-center/boosting-compliance-to-increase-freight-visibility-and-control-with-lipsey-logistics
(6) Verisk CargoNet 2024 cargo theft — $454.9M total, $202,364 per incident: https://www.verisk.com/company/newsroom/cargo-theft-surges-to-record-levels-in-2024-verisk-cargonet-analysis-reveals/
(7) CargoNet Q2 2024 theft trends — 771 incidents Q2 2024, 33% increase vs Q2 2023: https://www.cargonet.com/news-and-events/cargonet-in-the-media/2024-q2-theft-trends/
(8) FMCSA 2024 Pocket Guide — carrier fleet size distribution, 97% operate ≤20 trucks: https://www.fmcsa.dot.gov/sites/fmcsa.dot.gov/files/2025-09/FMCSA%20Pocket%20Guide%202024-v6%20508%20.pdf
(9) project44 G2 Winter 2024 Report — 93% data quality vs 88% category average: https://www.project44.com/blog/g2-winter-2024-report/
(10) Wiser LogTech — RTTVP reliability critique including Flexport manual data entry: https://www.wiserlogtech.com/rttvp-is-now-scorched-earth/
