### Pain point 3.2: IoT sensor data is siloed to the buyer of the device — no shared data layer across the journey

**Description:** When a shipper deploys Tive, Roambee, or Sensitech TempTale sensors on a shipment, the sensor data belongs to the shipper. The 3PL, the freight forwarder, and the cargo insurer on the same shipment have no API access to that telemetry unless a bilateral data-sharing agreement has been executed separately. In the absence of that agreement — which is the norm, not the exception — the cargo insurer prices the policy on declared risk parameters, the 3PL manages exceptions via manual alerts forwarded by the shipper, and the insurer learns about excursions only at claim time. This silo is structural: there is no neutral IoT data exchange for freight. Parsyl's Data Partner Program (launched March 2024) represents the first commercial attempt to create a consent-based bridge between monitoring vendors (Tive, Roambee, Sensitech, Berlinger, Copeland) and a cargo insurer — but Parsyl is a single insurer with a small market share, and the program is opt-in, not default. The broader market of cargo underwriters at Lloyd's, AIG, Swiss Re, and Zurich have not announced equivalent programs.

**Who bears it:** Cargo insurers (price risk on undisclosed sensor data, bear claims they could have mitigated with early warning); shippers with temperature-sensitive cargo (pay higher premiums without IoT data benefit despite having deployed sensors); 3PLs intermediating shipments (responsible for excursion response but without automatic data access); underwriters of pharmaceutical and food cargo globally.

**Estimated cost:**
- **Insurance premium penalty without verified monitoring data:** Without verifiable monitoring data, temperature-sensitive cargo shippers pay 15–25% higher insurance premiums. For a pharmaceutical shipper with $10M in annual cargo insurance premium, this equals $1.5M–$2.5M/year in excess premium attributable to unverified risk. Cross-validate: insure24.co.uk quotes pharmaceutical cold chain premiums at 0.5–3% of insured values; a 15–30% risk management discount band would save $75K–$900K per $10M insured on the low rate, or $450K–$5.4M on the high rate. (1)(2)
- **Cold chain product loss from delayed excursion response:** Cold chain cargo losses attributable to temperature excursions total billions annually. A 2025 IATA estimate put pharmaceutical air cargo losses from temperature excursions at $35B/year globally; WHO estimates 25% of vaccines are damaged due to cold chain failures. The silo means that even when sensors detect an excursion, the insurer and 3PL are not notified in real-time, and the intervention window closes. [ASSUMED-1]: In the absence of real-time insurer notification, the average response lag after a temperature excursion alert reaches the shipper's QA team and is escalated to the 3PL is 2–4 hours — a window in which cold-chain intervention (re-icing, rerouting, emergency storage) is often still possible but rarely taken. To validate: ask operations managers at three pharmaceutical 3PLs (DHL Supply Chain, UPS Healthcare, Cencora) how long it takes from a Sensitech or Controlant excursion alert to a documented intervention decision, and how often the cargo is ultimately salvaged vs. written off.
- **Actuarial mispricing from data asymmetry:** Shippers deploying IoT sensors and maintaining clean excursion histories have better risk profiles than their declared risk suggests — but insurers cannot verify this without data access. Parsyl's data partner program explicitly creates "improved coverage and beneficial rates" for customers who consent to share data, implying the current rate for non-sharing customers is above actuarially fair. The insurer-side cost is actuarial mispricing that systematically overcharges low-risk shippers and undercharges high-risk ones. (3)
- **Tive market scale as proxy for unshared data volume:** Tive has sold 4M+ trackers, logged nearly 12 billion miles, and serves 1,200+ customers; they reached $100M booked ARR in May 2026. Every Tive shipment generates a sensor data stream that, absent a Parsyl-type agreement, goes only to the shipper. At 4M+ trackers and thousands of active shipments daily, the volume of siloed, insurance-relevant telemetry is enormous. (4)

**Willingness to pay:** Not sourced for neutral IoT data exchange platform specifically. Parsyl's Data Partner Program creates premium discounts as a WTP proxy — the fact that shippers consent to share data in exchange for lower rates implies the rate benefit exceeds the cost of data consent for motivated shippers. [Interview placeholder: ask a Lloyd's cargo underwriter covering pharmaceutical cargo whether they would pay for an API feed of Sensitech or Tive data for active shipments, and what rate benefit they would pass through to data-sharing shippers.]

**Frequency:**
- Parsyl Data Partner Program (March 2024) covers five monitoring vendors (Berlinger, Copeland, Roambee, Sensitech, Tive) — but is a single insurer's opt-in initiative. No equivalent program exists at Lloyd's, AIG, Swiss Re, Zurich, or the broader cargo insurance market as of May 2026. (3)(5)
- Tive: 4M+ trackers sold, 1,200+ customers, $100M booked ARR (May 2026) — all data siloed to buying shipper absent consent agreement. (4)
- 15–25% premium penalty for temperature-sensitive shippers without verifiable monitoring data — cited across multiple independent insurance industry sources. (1)
- WHO estimate: 25% of vaccines arrive degraded due to cold chain failures; Controlant demonstrated <0.1% product loss for Pfizer COVID vaccines when end-to-end IoT monitoring was deployed across 5B+ doses — the actuarial gap between 25% and 0.1% is traceable precisely to data access. (6)
- The **IQVIA Institute for Human Data Science** (via the *2019 Biopharma Cold Chain Sourcebook* published by Pharmaceutical Commerce) estimates global biopharma cold-chain product loss at approximately **$35B/year** across all modes — the headline industry figure widely cited as "IATA $35B" is a misattribution; IATA's own published range for **air-cargo-specific temperature excursion losses is $2.5B–$12.5B**. Industry-wide IoT penetration is rising but insurer data integration remains absent. (7)(7a)

**Evidenced or assumed:**
(1) TagoIO cold chain IoT — 15–25% premium penalty without verifiable monitoring data: https://tago.io/blog/iot-game-changing-in-cold-chain
(7) IQVIA Institute via Pharmaceutical Commerce 2019 Biopharma Cold Chain Sourcebook — $35B annual biopharma cold-chain product-loss estimate; primary attribution confirmed in Pelican BioThermal 2019 Survey: https://www.pelicanbiothermal.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/2019-biopharma-cold-chain-logistics-survey.pdf ; original Pharmaceutical Commerce coverage: https://www.pharmaceuticalcommerce.com/
