### Pain point 6.4: Cargo theft recovery networks are structurally absent from SEA — no CargoNet or Overhaul equivalent operates in the region, and recovery is reactive and uncoordinated

**Description:** In North America, organized cargo theft recovery operates through two complementary systems: CargoNet (Verisk subsidiary), a shared theft intelligence network with law enforcement integration that tracks incident data across the US and Canada; and Overhaul, an integrated cargo risk + recovery platform that combines real-time GPS covert tracking, law enforcement liaison, and an exclusive insurance program. Overhaul claims a 99.9% shipment production rate (i.e., intact cargo arrival for monitored loads) and executes cross-jurisdiction recoveries. Neither CargoNet nor Overhaul operates in SEA. The recovery infrastructure gap means that cargo theft in Indonesia, Thailand, and Malaysia relies on: (a) local police reports filed after delivery failure (typically 12–48 hours after theft); (b) informal carrier networks for any intelligence sharing; and (c) Tokio Marine, MSIG, or Sompo's own claims investigation teams, which are claims-assessment functions, not real-time recovery operations. Strait of Malacca piracy in 2025 surged to a 19-year high (108 incidents; 281% YoY increase), yet no dedicated cargo theft recovery network with law enforcement API integration operates in the region. BSI/TT Club 2025 places Indonesia in the global top 9 for cargo theft, with insider involvement elevated above global average.

**Who bears it:** SEA cargo shippers (bear total loss on stolen goods); SEA cargo insurers (Tokio Marine, MSIG, Sompo — pay indemnity with near-zero recovery expectation); regional 3PLs (lose shipper relationships after theft events); logistics-intensive industries (electronics, pharmaceutical, FMCG, automotive parts — the highest-value theft targets).

**Estimated cost:**
- **SEA cargo theft losses:** No published aggregate dollar figure equivalent to CargoNet's $725M US estimate exists for SEA road freight. BSI/TT Club 2025 confirms Indonesia is top-9 globally by incident volume; Asia-region cargo theft is documented but not monetized in the same way as US data. [ASSUMED-8]: Annual cargo theft losses in Indonesia + Malaysia + Thailand combined exceed $500M (US equivalent basis), given Indonesia's top-9 global ranking and the absence of any recovery network that might suppress losses — to validate: ask Tokio Marine Indonesia, MSIG Malaysia, and Sompo Singapore for aggregate cargo theft claims paid in the most recent fiscal year.
- **Recovery rate gap:** Overhaul's 99.9% intact-arrival rate for actively monitored US shipments vs. the industry baseline (no equivalent published for SEA, but assumed below 20% based on absence of recovery infrastructure and reliance on reactive local police response). [ASSUMED-3 from Pain point 6.1 applies here].
- **Maritime piracy cost:** 108 Strait of Malacca piracy incidents in 2025 (19-year high); engine parts stolen in 31% of incidents; full cargo theft incidents not separately counted at the total value level in public sources. Piracy incidents trigger war-risk and piracy endorsements in marine cargo policies, increasing insurance costs for SEA maritime routes. (1)(2)
- **Recovery network subscription value:** CargoNet charges annual membership fees to carriers and law enforcement agencies; Overhaul charges a platform fee plus success fees. Neither publicly discloses pricing. The absence of a SEA equivalent is both a market gap and an unquantified premium to Tokio Marine's claims book (total-loss claims that would have been partial recoveries in a functioning recovery ecosystem).

**Willingness to pay:** Not sourced. [ASSUMED-9]: SEA cargo shippers and insurers would pay an annual subscription of $5,000–$50,000 for access to a regional cargo theft intelligence and recovery coordination network analogous to CargoNet, calibrated by shipment volume and cargo value — to validate: ask supply chain security managers at five SEA-headquartered FMCG or pharmaceutical companies whether they currently subscribe to any cargo theft intelligence service and what they would pay for one.

**Frequency:**
- Indonesia top-9 globally for cargo theft (BSI/TT Club 2025, April 2026 report). (3)
- Truck-based theft accounts for ~70% of all global cargo theft incidents (BSI/TT Club 2025). (3)
- 22% of cargo theft incidents globally involve insider cooperation — in Asia specifically, "insider involvement remains particularly high across India, China, and Indonesia, often involving gradual pilferage supported by weak inventory controls and internal collusion" (BSI/TT Club 2025). (3)
- Strait of Malacca piracy: 108 incidents in 2025, 281% YoY surge, highest 19-year level (ReCAAP ISC data, 2025). (1)(2)
- Overhaul Q1 2026 — 99.9% shipment production rate for monitored loads vs. 574 theft incidents total in US in Q1 alone. No SEA equivalent monitoring program exists. (4)
- Recovery rates collapse sharply after 48 hours; detection often delayed 12–48 hours in absence of real-time GPS alert (see Pain point 6.1, RC3). In SEA, where GPS coverage is thinner and police response slower, the effective recovery window is structurally shorter.

**Evidenced or assumed:**
(1) Maritime Mutual — Strait of Malacca piracy attacks continue into 2025; 19-year high: https://maritime-mutual.com/risk-bulletins/straits-of-malacca-and-singapore-piracy-armed-robbery-attacks-continue-into-2025/
(2) Ship & Bunker — Malacca piracy 19-year high in 2025, 108 incidents: https://shipandbunker.com/news/apac/866169-piracy-in-the-strait-of-malacca-surged-to-a-19-year-high-in-2025 ; World Ports Organization: https://www.worldports.org/piracy-in-strait-of-malacca-singapore-strait-surges-nearly-fourfold-in-2025-says-anti-piracy-watchdog/
(3) BSI Consulting / TT Club 2025 Cargo Theft Report (April 2026 edition) — Indonesia top-9, 22% insider involvement, 70% truck-based, Asia insider prevalence elevated: https://www.ttclub.com/news-and-resources/news/article/tt-club-bsi-consulting-warn-of-escalating-global-cargo-theft-as-criminal-tactics-grow-increasingly-sophisticated ; BSI press release: https://www.bsigroup.com/en-US/insights-and-media/media-center/press-releases/2026/april/tt-club-and-bsi-report-rise-in-cargo-theft-as-criminal-tactics-evolve/
(4) Overhaul Q1 2026 — 99.9% production rate, 574 incidents, 31% deceptive pickup surge: https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/us-cargo-theft-dipped-in-q1-2026-while-deceptive-pickup-schemes-jumped-31-overhaul-report-finds-302773974.html
(5) CargoNet (Verisk) 2025 full-year statistics — $725M losses, $273,990 average theft value, no SEA operational presence: https://www.verisk.com/company/newsroom/cargo-theft-losses-surge-to-estimated-$725-million-in-2025-verisk-cargonet-analysis-reveals/ ; https://www.cargonet.com/
(6) Overhaul $105M Series C, August 2025; US/Brazil/Mexico/Czech Republic offices only (no SEA): https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/overhaul-secures-105-million-series-c-to-drive-platform-innovation-and-expansion-302533989.html
(7) FreightWaves — $4M cargo theft recovery showing law enforcement coordination value: https://www.freightwaves.com/news/4-million-cargo-theft-recovery-shows-what-enforcement-can-do
(8) Geotab 2026 cargo theft cyber era report — $6.6B total losses, 41% GPS fleet adoption: https://www.geotab.com/press-release/cargo-theft-blueprint-report/
(9) Payload Asia — APAC cargo theft hotspots: Indonesia, India, China high-risk: https://payloadasia.com/2021/02/report-shows-high-risk-countries-for-cargo-theft-in-asia/
(10) Statista — APAC distribution of cargo theft by country (Indonesia 14% of Asia-region incidents): https://www.statista.com/statistics/1156158/apac-distribution-of-cargo-theft-by-country/
(11) ReCAAP ISC — piracy information sharing centre, Singapore (sea-lane piracy data but no road freight recovery network): https://www.recaap.org/

**Root Causes**

- **RC1: CargoNet's model is built on a shared intelligence network funded by member carriers and insurers who have a direct financial incentive to contribute data — a collective-action model that has never been attempted in SEA.** CargoNet works because US insurance companies, carriers, and law enforcement agencies share theft data on a common platform, creating network effects where every report improves the intelligence available to every member. This model requires: (a) insurers with cargo theft claims data willing to share it on an anonymized basis; (b) carriers willing to report theft incidents rather than absorb losses silently to avoid rate increases; and (c) law enforcement agencies with integration APIs. None of these conditions exist in SEA at scale. SEA cargo insurers hold claims data as proprietary; carriers fear rate retaliation; police forces do not have cargo-theft-specific API integration with commercial platforms. Building the SEA equivalent requires a convener with insurer credibility — a role that Tokio Marine or a Tokio Marine–backed venture could occupy.

- **RC2: Theft recovery in SEA depends on local police forces that are not equipped, incentivized, or networked for commercial cargo theft response at speed.** In North America, law enforcement agencies (FBI, State Police, local departments with dedicated cargo crime units) participate in CargoNet's notification system and respond to cargo theft incidents as priority matters, particularly for high-value loads. In Indonesia, Thailand, and Malaysia, cargo theft is managed by general police forces without dedicated cargo crime units or industry intelligence-sharing arrangements. Police response to theft reports typically takes 24–72 hours for a standard incident; the 48-hour critical recovery window has almost certainly closed before any recovery operation begins. The structural cause is resource allocation: police agencies in SEA prioritize violent crime, not property crime in commercial freight.

- **RC3: The absence of standardized cargo GPS tracking in SEA's informal carrier tier means there is no recoverable asset to track after a theft is reported.** Even if a recovery network existed in SEA, it could only mobilize covert GPS recovery for loads where a tracking device had been deployed before the theft. In the US, Overhaul's recovery model relies on covert GPS devices placed in shipments — devices that thieves are unaware of and cannot remove. In SEA's informal carrier tier, shipper-deployed GPS tracking is the exception rather than the rule (GPS tracker penetration among non-large-fleet SEA road freight operators is estimated below 20%). Without a covert tracking device in the load, recovery must rely on informant networks and police patrols — an approach with near-zero recovery rates for organized cargo theft.

- **RC4: The structural fragmentation of SEA cargo theft data across national police systems, insurer claims files, and shipper incident reports prevents the formation of pattern intelligence that would enable proactive intervention.** CargoNet's predictive value comes from its longitudinal dataset of 15+ years of US theft incidents, which allows it to identify corridor-specific patterns, commodity targeting cycles, and organized ring behaviors. No equivalent dataset exists for SEA road freight. The BSI/TT Club annual report is the closest publicly available resource — but it aggregates data at the country level, not the corridor, commodity, or carrier level that would enable actionable pre-dispatch intelligence. Data aggregation across SEA's five-country market would require a platform-level data-sharing arrangement that crosses national regulatory jurisdictions — a significant governance challenge that no current player has addressed.

**SEA whitespace framing:** A Tokio Marine-anchored cargo theft intelligence and recovery network for SEA — starting in Indonesia and Thailand (highest incident volume), with digital freight platform (Kargo Technologies, Ezyhaul) carrier GPS data as the input layer and Tokio Marine's claims database as the intelligence backbone — would be the regional first-mover in recovery network infrastructure. The venture does not need to replicate CargoNet's full model; a minimum viable version is a shared API that allows Tokio Marine's SEA claims teams to see live GPS pings for insured loads, trigger law enforcement notification within 30 minutes of geofence breach, and share anonymized theft incident data across the Tokio Marine SEA entity network. This is buildable with existing IoT and GPS infrastructure; the gap is the coordination layer and the insurer data-sharing governance.

