### Hypotheses tested — Stage 6 outcomes

**WH4 (incident response is reactive and slow — claims platforms shorten payout, not response time): Confirmed and refined.** Claims platforms (Loadsure, Five Sigma, Parsyl) have measurably shortened settlement timelines in the US and Lloyd's market. The hypothesis is correct that these platforms do not shorten detection-to-FNOL time, which is the structural cap on recovery value. The detection gap is documented (Pain point 6.1) and structurally explained through three root causes. SEA is structurally worse than the US on both dimensions.

**WH5 (insurance pricing disconnected from real journey data — parametric thin outside specialty): Confirmed and amplified.** Telematics-native pricing exists commercially only in US trucking (Nirvana). Parametric cargo products exist only at Lloyd's (Parsyl, AXA Climate) and are entirely absent from SEA commercial distribution. The gap is larger than the hypothesis implied: it is not just parametric that is thin — IoT-linked underwriting of any kind is essentially absent from SEA cargo. The Tokio Marine Future Fund → Nirvana investment creates an internal knowledge asset that the venture can transfer.

**Subrogation across sub-contractor tier is broken: Confirmed.** Pain point 6.3 fully documents the FAAAA preemption dead-end, the Carmack liability cap dynamics, the waiver-of-subrogation prevalence, and the SEA amplification (no statutory liability framework). The hypothesis understated the role of fictitious carrier entities (deceptive pickup schemes) as a structural subrogation eliminator — when the carrier is a fraud entity, there is no subrogation target at all.

**Theft recovery rates below 50% in US/LATAM; SEA has no recovery network: Confirmed for SEA absence; partially confirmed for US rates.** CargoNet does not publish a recovery rate; Overhaul's 99.9% intact-arrival for monitored loads and the Geotab 73% vs <20% alert-timing differential are the best available proxies. SEA's absence of a recovery network is fully confirmed and documented as Pain point 6.4.

**SEA: cargo insurance bundled in 3PL contracts, claims data not shared, parametric thin: Confirmed.** Documented in Pain points 6.5 (parametric absent) and in the structural framing throughout. The 3PL bundled distribution model is confirmed as a structural barrier to parametric adoption in SEA (RC3 of Pain point 6.5). Claims data fragmentation is documented structurally.

**New pain point surfaced (not in hypotheses): Pain point 6.N1 — insurance claims loss data is structurally siloed from carrier vetting platforms.** This cross-stage structural failure was not in the working hypotheses. It closes the loop between Stage 1 (carrier vetting) and Stage 6 (claims outcomes) and represents a systemic market gap that makes the full value chain less self-correcting than it should be. It is flagged for orchestrator attention as a cross-stage finding.
